 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to providing notice regarding\nthe timeliness of the receipt by the board of elections of ballots capable of\nreturn by mail",
    "basePrintNo": "S10563",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10563-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §7-127, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10563\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to providing notice\nregarding the timeliness of the receipt by the board of elections of\nballots capable of return by mail\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure mail ballot voters have advance notice of the timeliness of\nthe receipt of mailed in ballots.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends the election law by adding a new section 7-127 estab-\nlishing that every mail ballot shall come with a written notice inform-\ning the voter of all the methods by which such ballot may be timely\nreturned. It clarifies that such notice will include a reminder of the\nimportance of seeking a hand cancellation date for any ballot returned\nby domestic postal mail to ensure the date of mailing can always be\ndetermined. This section additionally requires the inclusion of the\nwebsite and telephone number of the appropriate board of elections for\nthe relevant drop-off information in such notification. Finally, this\nsection enables the state board of elections to promulgate any rules and\nregulations necessary to implement the provisions of this section.\n\nSection 2: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent New York State law establishes that mail ballot voters may have\ntheir votes counted up to seven days past election day, so long as their\nballot is postmarked by election day. A new rule created by the United\nStates Postal Service (USPS) puts this practice at risk, as it estab-\nlishes that USPS does not have to postmark mail at the time when it is\nreceived. This has implications on whether mail ballot votes will count,\nas these postmarks serve to confirm whether these ballots were submitted\nand received before the polls close.\n\nWith such a significant change regarding the timing of when mail in\nballots can be received such that votes are counted, it is imperative\nthat all voters, mail ballot voters included, have an opportunity to\nexercise their civic duty to the fullest extent. This bill would do so\nby equipping mail ballot voters with the information they need so that\nthey can take the steps necessary to ensure that their ballots are\nreceived by election day and can thus have their votes counted.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
